1.  The points $T_h,T_{ha}$ are formal for this localization, $\ell_K(z(T_h))\ne0$, and $$\label{red:primary-test}
     \frac{\ell_K(z(T_{ha}))}{\ell_K(z(T_h))}-a
                                \in r^K(R_F)_{\mathfrak p}.$$

2.  The points $T_{j_0}^{\sigma},T_{(h-4)a}^{\sigma}$ are formal for the conjugate curve at the same localization, and $$\label{red:alternate-test}
     \frac{z(T_{(h-4)a}^{\sigma})}{z(T_{j_0}^{\sigma})}
                                \in r^K(R_F)_{\mathfrak p}.$$

The sets $B,J$ are disjoint and avoid $2,3$. Moreover, $$\label{par:mutual-squares}
 \theta\in\mathbb Q_q^{\times2}\quad(q\in B),\qquad
 \beta\in\mathbb Q_q^{\times2}\quad(q\in J).$$ Both $\beta$ and $\theta$ are squares in $\mathbb Q_2^\times$; at $3$, $\theta$ is a square and $\beta$ is a unit nonsquare. In particular $B\ne\varnothing$.

Suppose that its support $Q$ contains the nonempty set $B$ of Lemma <a href="#par:cross-squares" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="par:cross-squares">15</a> and a prime outside $B$, and suppose that $$\label{par:matrix-target}
 \ker K=\langle\mathbf1,\mathbf1_B\rangle,
 \qquad \lambda\notin\operatorname{im}K.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
There is a fixed finite set of rational primes $S_E$ with the following properties. Let $p\notin S_E$, let $v$ be any place of $F$ above $p$, normalized by $v(p)=1$, and let $n,n'\in\mathbb Z$, with $n'\ne0$. Assume $nP,n'P$ reduce to $O$ at $v$. Then $$\label{ell:ratio}
           v\!\left(\frac{z(nP)}{z(n'P)}\right)=v_p(n/n').$$ For every integer $K\ge1$, if in addition $p>K+1$ and $v_p(n/n')\ge0$, then $\ell_K(z(n'P))\ne0$ and $$\label{ell:truncation}
 v\!\left(\frac{\ell_K(z(nP))}{\ell_K(z(n'P))}-\frac n{n'}\right)
                         \ge K.$$ Both assertions also hold at the same place $v$ after replacing $E,P,\ell_K$ by $E^\sigma,P^\sigma,\ell_K^\sigma$ and imposing the corresponding reduction-to-$O$ hypotheses on the conjugate points. Here and below the valuation of zero is $+\infty$, so $n=0$ is included.

There exist five distinct points $\mathcal C\subset\mathbf P^1(\mathbb Q)$, including $\infty$, a finite set $S$ of rational primes, and constants $H,c>0$ with the following property. For each $b\in\mathcal C$, choose a primitive integral linear form $L_b(U,V)$ vanishing at $b$, with $L_\infty(U,V)=V$. For $s=(u:v)\in\mathbf P^1(\mathbb Q)\setminus\mathcal C$, let $M_S(s)$ be the product, taken without repetition, of primes $q\notin S$ for which at least one of the integers $$\label{hei:radical}
 v_q(L_b(u,v))-\min\{v_q(u),v_q(v)\},\qquad b\in\mathcal C,$$ is odd. Here $v_q(q)=1$ and $v_q(0)=+\infty$. Then $$\label{hei:height-bound}
                    h(s)\le H M_S(s)^c.$$

For the prime $q$ and element $r$ in <a href="#red:odd-r" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="red:odd-r">[red:odd-r]</a>, there is a branch $\mathfrak p\in\{\mathfrak p_+,\mathfrak p_-\}$ of that representation axiom and an ambient place $v_*$ of ${}^*F$ over $q$ such that, for every ordinary integer $l\ge1$ and every $D'\in F_R$, $$\label{red:valuation-transfer}
 D'\in r^l(R_F)_{\mathfrak p}
               \quad\Longrightarrow\quad v_*(\widetilde D')\ge l.$$ Every point formal for this tested localization actually reduces to $O$ for $v_*$.

The sets $B,J$ are disjoint and avoid $2,3$. Moreover, $$\label{par:mutual-squares}
 \theta\in\mathbb Q_q^{\times2}\quad(q\in B),\qquad
 \beta\in\mathbb Q_q^{\times2}\quad(q\in J).$$ Both $\beta$ and $\theta$ are squares in $\mathbb Q_2^\times$; at $3$, $\theta$ is a square and $\beta$ is a unit nonsquare. In particular $B\ne\varnothing$.

Let $l>0$ be squarefree, prime to $6$, with $l\equiv3\pmod4$ and $l\equiv2\pmod3$. Suppose that its support $Q$ contains the nonempty set $B$ of Lemma <a href="#par:cross-squares" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="par:cross-squares">15</a> and a prime outside $B$, and suppose that $$\label{par:matrix-target}
 \ker K=\langle\mathbf1,\mathbf1_B\rangle,
 \qquad \lambda\notin\operatorname{im}K.$$ Then $$\dim_{\mathbb F_2}\mathop{\mathrm{Sel}}_2(E_l/\mathbb Q)=3,$$ and $(\beta,1)$ is a Selmer class outside the subgroup of rational two-torsion classes, in Kummer coordinates $(x+3l,x-l)$.

There is a finite Dirichlet character $\chi$ of order $m\le C M$, unramified outside $T$, and a continuous cochain $\beta:G_\mathbb Q\to\mu_{2m}$ such that $$\label{hei:beta}
 \beta^2=\chi,\qquad
 \frac{\beta(g)\beta(h)}{\beta(gh)}=\epsilon(g,h).$$ The cochain can be chosen trivial on inertia at every prime outside $T$. Its restriction to $G_L$ is a conjugacy-invariant character. The local conductor of $\chi$ at any fixed prime is bounded independently of $s$, and $\chi$ is tame at odd primes.

There is a finite set $\mathcal K\subset\mathbb Z_{>0}$ such that, for every $A\in\mathbb Z$ and every real $B>0$, there exist integers $u_1,u_2,v$, with $v\ne0$, for which, on setting $u_3=Av-u_1-u_2$, each of the thirteen numbers $$\label{pri:forms-list}
       v,\qquad L_b(u_j,v)
       \quad(1\le j\le3,\ b\in\mathcal C\setminus\{\infty\})$$ has the form $\epsilon kr$, where $\epsilon\in\{1,-1\}$, $k\in\mathcal K$, and $r>B$ is a positive rational prime. Every such prime splits in $F$ and is a prime of good reduction for both $E$ and $E^\sigma$. At either place of $F$ above $r$, if $d_1,d_2$ are the cardinalities of the reduction groups of $E,E^\sigma$ over $\mathbb F_r$, then $$\label{pri:order-conditions}
                    \gcd(d_1,d_2)\mid4,
                    \qquad r\nmid d_1d_2.$$ The set $\mathcal K$ is independent of $A$ and $B$.

The number of points in this square avoiding every $\Omega_p$ is at most $$\label{pri:sieve-bound}
            \frac{n^2}{G(Z)}+O(nZ^6+Z^8),\qquad
 G(Z)=\sum_{\substack{e\le Z\text{ squarefree}\\p\mid e\Rightarrow p\in\mathcal P_Z}}
                           \prod_{p\mid e}\frac{g(p)}{1-g(p)}.$$ The error constant is absolute.
